Question Id: 515131
Q 04: Q 04: A 30-year-old man presents with intense nocturnal pruritus and multiple excoriated papules in the web spaces of his fingers. Physical examination confirms the presence of burrows, consistent with uncomplicated scabies. He has no other comorbidities. Considering efficacy, safety, and current treatment guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate first-line therapy? (Question Id: 515131)
Oral ivermectin (single dose)
Benzyl benzoate lotion
Lindane shampoo
Sulfur ointment
Permethrin 5% cream
ANSWER: E

Keywords in the Stem to identify correct option 
“Uncomplicated scabies” – Indicates standard, healthy adult case, guiding toward first-line topical therapy rather than systemic or toxic options.
“No comorbidities” – Confirms safety profile is not restricted, supporting use of permetrin cream.
“Burrows in web spaces” – Classic pathognomonic sign of scabies; confirms typical presentation requiring standard therapy.
Classic triad (if any)
Nocturnal pruritus + burrows in finger webs + papular lesions → This is the classic triad of uncomplicated scabies.
These keywords directly point to Permethrin 5% cream as the first-line treatment.

Explanation 

(Option E) Permethrin 5% cream:
Permethrin is a synthetic pyrethroid that acts as a neurotoxin to the mites. It works by disrupting the sodium channel currents on the nerve cell membranes of the parasites, leading to delayed repolarization, paralysis, and death of the scabies mite.
Permethrin 5% cream is globally recognized as the Gold Standard and first-line treatment for uncomplicated scabies in adults and children (two months and older).


Application: It must be applied thoroughly from the neck down to the soles of the feet and left on for 8–14 hours (usually overnight) before being washed off.
Safety & Efficacy: It has a very low rate of systemic absorption and a high cure rate (often >90% after a single application, though a second application one week later is frequently recommended to kill mites that hatched from surviving eggs).
Why it's preferred: Compared to other options, it has a superior safety profile, lacks the neurological toxicity of Lindane, and is more effective than sulfur or benzyl benzoate.
System involved: Integumentary system (skin).

(Option A) Oral ivermectin (single dose):
Use: Second-line or for crusted scabies or outbreaks; not first-line for uncomplicated scabies.
Advantageous in patients who cannot use topical therapy.
Mechanism: Paralyzes and kills the mites via chloride channel modulation.
Less preferred due to lower data on single-dose efficacy for uncomplicated cases.
(Option B) Benzyl benzoate lotion:
Use: Alternative topical therapy, effective but more irritating to the skin.
Often used in resource-limited settings.
Requires repeated application for full efficacy.
(Option C) Lindane shampoo:
Use: Previously common, now restricted due to neurotoxicity risk, especially in children and pregnant women.
Not first-line due to potential systemic toxicity (seizures, aplastic anemia rare).
(Option D) Sulfur ointment:
Use: Safe in infants, pregnant women, and in resource-limited areas.
Less effective, unpleasant odor, and requires multiple days of application.
Rarely used in routine adult therapy.

Key Concept 
Permethrin 5% cream is the gold-standard first-line therapy for uncomplicated scabies in adults.
Choice of therapy depends on efficacy, safety, and patient factors.
Topical therapy is preferred; systemic therapy is reserved for special situations like crusted scabies or outbreaks.


Subject: Medicine (Internal)
System: Dermatology (Integumentary system) – Infestations & Infections – Scabies - First-Line Management
Topic: Scabies – Uncomplicated (Dermatology/Infectious Skin Infestation)

Competency Domain: Management
Cognitive Level: Application
Clinical Skill: Decision-making
Difficulty Level: Easy


Scabies – Uncomplicated 
(Dermatology/Infectious Skin Infestation)
Feature
Description
Clinical Relevance / Notes
Etiology
• Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis
• Human-specific mite, transmitted via close contact
Transmission
• Direct skin-to-skin contact 
• Fomites (less common)
• High risk in households, crowded settings
Clinical Features
• Intense nocturnal pruritus 
• Burrows in web spaces, wrists, waist, genitals 
• Papular eruptions, excoriations
• Classic triad: nocturnal pruritus + burrows + papules
Diagnosis
• Clinical examination 
• Identification of burrows 
• Dermoscopy or skin scraping (microscopy)
• Mainly clinical; microscopy confirms diagnosis if uncertain
Treatment – First-line
• Permethrin 5% cream 
• Apply neck-to-toe overnight 
• Repeat after 1 week
• Safe, highly effective, standard guideline therapy
Alternative/
Second-line
• Oral ivermectin (systemic) 
• Benzyl benzoate lotion 
• Sulfur ointment • Lindane (restricted use)
• Used in special cases: crusted scabies, outbreaks, or topical intolerance
Prevention
• Treat close contacts simultaneously
 • Wash clothes, bedding in hot water • 
Avoid prolonged skin-to-skin contact during treatment
• Prevents reinfestation and household outbreaks
Complications
• Secondary bacterial infection (impetigo) 
• Post-scabetic eczema
• Usually mild in uncomplicated cases



Differential Diagnosis of
Uncomplicated Scabies 
(Dermatology/Infectious Skin Infestation)
Condition
Key Features
Distinguishing Points from Scabies
Atopic Dermatitis
• Chronic pruritus 
• Eczema-like patches 
• Flexural involvement 
• Lichenification
• Usually chronic, family history of atopy 
• No burrows 
• Pruritus not specifically nocturnal
Contact Dermatitis
• Localized pruritus 
• Erythema, vesicles 
• History of exposure to irritants/allergens
• Confined to exposed area 
• No burrows 
• Acute onset after exposure
Papular Urticaria
• Papular lesions 
• Pruritus 
• Often in children 
• History of insect bites
• Lesions clustered on exposed areas 
• Burrows absent 
• Self-limiting
Pediculosis 
(Head/Body Lice)
• Intense itching 
• Nits or lice visible 
• Affects scalp or body
• Lesions confined to hair-bearing areas 
• No burrows on web spaces 
• Transmission pattern differs
Dermatitis 
Herpetiformis
• Intensely pruritic papulovesicles 
• Symmetric distribution on extensor surfaces 
• Associated with celiac disease
• Vesicles rather than burrows 
• Chronic relapsing course 
• Gluten-related triggers
Psoriasis 
(Guttate or Plaque)
• Erythematous scaly plaques 
• Well-demarcated 
• Often extensor surfaces
• Minimal pruritus compared to scabies 
• No burrows 
• Chronic course, family history common


